From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] An application that has not drawn yet is not an application that
 has gone away

Patch 0069 was run on the device the hour it was written (2026-11-03,
eng.builde.20261103.020021) and it did nothing. Both arms of its own comparison
-- the behaviour before it, and the behaviour with it -- were the same black
screen, and mHeldOverClosingApps never appeared in a dump.

The lever it shipped for reaching the branch worked:
debug.pw3.app_transition_timeout_ms brought the deadline down to 2 s, and at
2.3 s dumpsys showed the previous screen already gone
(com.android.launcher3 isOnScreen=false) with no window for the incoming
application in the list at all. So the branch was reached and the hold was not
kept.

The cause is one line of 0069:

    gone &= wtoken.findMainWindow() == null;

That is "this application has no window", which is true of an application that
has lost its windows and equally true of one that has not made them yet -- and
the second is exactly the application a hold is taken for. So the hold was taken
at the deadline and dropped as "gone" on the very next surface placement, before
anything could be seen. The screen was then hidden by the ordinary path, which
is why it looked like the patch was not in the image.

What is asked instead is whether the window manager still has the token:
removed, mIsExiting, or no display content. All three are set when an activity
really goes away, and none of them is set by an activity that is merely slow,
which is the distinction that was missing.

The second change is why this took a rebuild to find rather than a line of log.
0069 added no log at all, so what the device had to say about it was a
screenshot and a dump of a set that is only printed when it is not empty -- that
is, the evidence for "it held" and the evidence for "it held and let go
immediately" were the same evidence. Two Slog.i lines are added: one where the
hold is taken, naming how many screens are kept and how many applications are
waited for, and one where it is let go, saying which of the two reasons applied.
A transition that misses its deadline is rare enough that this is not a log that
repeats.

Not run on the device yet when this patch was written.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/WindowSurfacePlacer.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/WindowSurfacePlacer.java
@@ -557,6 +557,9 @@
                 return false;
             }
         }
+        Slog.i(TAG, "PW3: the deadline passed with nothing to show; keeping "
+                + mService.mClosingApps.size() + " screen(s) until "
+                + mService.mOpeningApps.size() + " application(s) draw");
         return true;
     }
 
@@ -564,6 +567,19 @@
         return wtoken.allDrawn || wtoken.startingDisplayed;
     }
 
+    /* Has this application gone away, as opposed to not having drawn yet?
+     *
+     * Patch 0069 asked findMainWindow() == null, which is true of both, and an
+     * application that has not made its window yet is exactly the one the hold
+     * is for: on the device (2026-11-03) the hold was taken at the deadline and
+     * dropped on the very next surface placement as "gone", so the previous
+     * screen was never kept and the arm with the patch looked the same as the
+     * arm without it. What is asked instead is whether the window manager still
+     * has the token at all. */
+    private static boolean isGone(AppWindowToken wtoken) {
+        return wtoken.removed || wtoken.mIsExiting || wtoken.getDisplayContent() == null;
+    }
+
     /* Let go of the applications held on the screen past a deadline.
      *
      * Released -- hidden for real -- on the first surface placement in which
@@ -588,11 +604,13 @@
         for (int i = 0; i < waitingCount; i++) {
             final AppWindowToken wtoken = mService.mHeldOverOpeningApps.valueAt(i);
             drawn &= hasSomethingOnScreen(wtoken);
-            gone &= wtoken.findMainWindow() == null;
+            gone &= isGone(wtoken);
         }
         if (!drawn && !gone) {
             return;
         }
+        Slog.i(TAG, "PW3: letting go of " + mService.mHeldOverClosingApps.size()
+                + " held screen(s): " + (drawn ? "the application drew" : "the application went away"));
         if (drawn) {
             for (int i = mService.mHeldOverClosingApps.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
